course: politics
lesson: 33
title: Cherry-picked clips and clickbait headlines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A short clip can be made of real words and still tell a false story, because what was cut out can flip the meaning.
explain: Imagine someone records you saying "I hate broccoli", but cuts off the start, "My little brother says", and shows it to your gran: every word is yours, and the story is still wrong.
explain: Old videos get new captions, photos get cropped so a crowd looks tiny or huge, and one trip or one laugh is made to stand for the whole person.
explain: Clickbait headlines shout and hide the facts so you click, and headlines with a scary question often have the answer "no" inside.
explain: The fix is to find the whole thing: the full speech, the real page, the wider photo, the story under the headline.
fact: Cutting a clip so its meaning changes is unfair, even if every word in it is real; this is taking it out of context.
fact: Old videos are often shared with new captions as if they were happening now; this is called recycled footage.
fact: Clickbait is a headline made to get clicks rather than to tell the news, and websites earn money from the adverts you see when you click.
fact: Headlines are short and often leave out the important "maybe" and "only" that the story underneath says.
fact: Cropping a photo can make a crowd look small or huge, which is why people argue about crowd sizes.
fact: Moments shown without what happened before can make anyone look bad, sometimes on purpose and sometimes by mistake.
fact: One clip shows one moment, not the whole person; choosing only the bits that fit is called cherry-picking.
fact: A big percentage can sound scary when the real numbers are tiny: up from 2 to 3 cases is "up 50%".
fact: A headline asking a scary question is not a fact; Betteridge's law is a joke rule that such questions can usually be answered "no".
fact: To check a clip, find the full event on an official channel or a news outlet and watch a minute before and after.
fact: A screenshot of a headline is easy to change; the real article on the outlet's own website is what counts.
fact: Words in this lesson: "mid" means average or not very good; "I'm dead" means "that's so funny"; "dreich" is Scottish for grey and wet weather; "yeah nah" in Australia and New Zealand means no.
exercise: A ten-second clip shows a politician saying "We should close all the parks". What might the full speech show? | Words before it that change the meaning, like "Some people say..." | Exactly the same ten seconds, nothing else, because politicians only ever speak in short bits | That the politician never gave any speech | That parks were closed the next morning
exercise: What does "out of context" mean? | Taken away from what came before and after, so the meaning changes | Filmed outside instead of inside a building, so the sound and the meaning are different | Shown on a different TV channel | Said too quietly for anyone to hear
exercise: A flood video says "happening now in our city", but Gran remembers it from years ago. What is it? | Recycled footage with a new caption | A live report from a news channel | A deepfake made by AI last night | A correction of an old story
exercise: "You won't BELIEVE what this MP said!" leads to a boring speech about bus timetables. What is the headline? | Clickbait | Breaking news | A correction | A fact-check
exercise: Why do websites use clickbait? | They earn money when you click and see adverts | They must use it by law | It is the only way to tell the facts | Readers pay extra for shouty headlines, so the sites earn more from each reader
exercise: The headline says "Chocolate is good for you" but the story says "a small amount, in a small study, maybe". What does that show? | Headlines can leave out the important "maybe" and "only" | The story is wrong and the headline is right | Chocolate is proven to be healthy in any amount, as much as you like | Headlines always say more than the story knows
exercise: One photo of a rally shows a small crowd; a wider one shows a huge crowd. What changed? | How much of the scene the photo shows | The number of people who came | The day the rally happened | The country the rally was in
exercise: A clip shows a reporter laughing during sad news, but the full video shows a dog ran into the shot. What does that teach? | A moment without its story can make anyone look bad | Reporters always laugh at sad news when the cameras are off, and this time they forgot | Dogs are not allowed in news studios | The full video was a deepfake
exercise: A video shows a politician tripping once. Does it prove he is too old to run the country? | No, one clip is one moment, not the whole person | Yes, one trip proves everything about a person and how they do their job | Yes, young people never trip | Yes, if the video is shared enough times
exercise: What is cherry-picking? | Choosing only the bits that fit what you want to show | Picking fruit carefully at a farm | Reading every source before you decide | Choosing the full video over a short clip
exercise: "Crime up 50%!" turns out to mean 2 cases became 3 in a small village. What question helps? | 50% of what? | Who is the reporter's favourite team? | What colour is the headline? | How many likes does it have?
exercise: A headline asks "Is your tap water poisoning you?" and the article says no. What is the lesson? | A scary question in a headline is not a fact | The water must be poisonous after all | Question headlines are always true | Articles never answer their headlines
exercise: How can Ethan check a viral clip of a speech? | Find the full speech on the official channel and watch around that part | Watch the same short clip ten more times, slowly, until you are sure what the politician meant | Read only the comments under the clip | Ask the account that shared the clip
exercise: A screenshot says a city is banning bikes, but the real site says it is adding bike lanes. What happened? | The screenshot was changed or made up | The city changed its mind twice in one day | The real website made a mistake about it | Bikes were banned and bike lanes added at once
exercise: What is a primary source for a speech? | The full speech itself | A short clip cut from it | A comment about it online | A meme with a quote from it
test: "yeah nah, that politician wants to shut every park, it's in the clip mate" — what should you do first? | Find the full speech and listen to what came before | Get angry and share it with your friends | Believe it, because the words are real and come from the politician's own mouth | Write to the politician to shout at them
test: In Scotland on a dreich day: "Pure flood in our town the now, look at this video!" Gran says it's from years ago. What is the best check? | Look for the date and whether local news reports it | Trust the caption, it says "now" | Share it fast in case it is real | Ask the person who posted it for more videos of the flood, to be sure
test: "I'm dead, he tripped on the stairs, he's way too old to be in charge" — what is the fair reply? | One trip is one moment, not proof of how he does his job | Yes, tripping once proves he cannot lead the country any more | Yes, real leaders never trip | Yes, because the clip went viral
test: Mia's friend says, "Crime's up 50%, our village is so dangerous now!" It went from 2 cases to 3. What is true? | The rise is real but tiny in real numbers | The village now has hundreds more crimes | Fifty new crimes happened last year | Crime fell, so the headline is a lie
test: What would happen if everyone read only headlines? | Stronger claims than the stories say would spread | Everyone would know the full story, because headlines say everything | Headlines would start saying "maybe" more | News sites would stop making money
test: "that headline's mid, just says 'Is your tap water poisoning you?' — so it IS, right?" What is the best answer? | A question is not a fact; read the article, which says it is safe | Yes, if a headline asks, the answer is always yes, or they would not ask | Yes, newspapers only ask about real dangers | Yes, all tap water is poisonous
test: In a cartoon, a sneaky fox cuts a video of the hero saying "I would never steal the cake" down to "steal the cake". What trick is that? | Taking words out of context | Writing a correction | Making a fact-check | A live report
test: A crowd photo looks tiny. The friend says, "Nobody came to that rally, look!" What is a good question? | Is this the full scene, or a cropped close-up? | How many likes does the photo have? | Who is the friend's favourite politician? | What colour were the signs?
test: "screenshot of the BBC says they're banning bikes, so it's legit innit" — how do you check? | Search the real BBC site for that headline | Trust it, a screenshot is the same as the page | Zoom in on the screenshot to see the letters | Share it and see if anyone complains about it
test: A boy says, "The full video is a waste of time, the clip has the real words." What is wrong with that? | Real words can still mislead when the parts around them are cut | Nothing, clips always show the whole meaning, because the editors pick the most important bit | Full videos are always fake | Real words can never be misleading
test: "You won't BELIEVE what she said!!" What is the best guess before you click? | The story may be smaller than the headline promises | The story must be the biggest news of the year | The headline was written by the politician | Headlines with capitals are always true
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the presentation of a statement "out of context"? | Its separation from the surrounding material such that its meaning is altered | Its broadcast upon a channel other than the original, such that its meaning is altered for a different audience | Its recording at a location outside a building | Its delivery at a volume insufficient to be heard
exam: It is given that recorded offences rose from two cases to three. Ascertain the percentage increase so described. | Fifty per cent | Thirty-three per cent | One hundred per cent | One hundred and fifty per cent
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain publishers employ headlines designed to provoke curiosity rather than to inform. | Revenue derived from the advertisements viewed upon each visit | A statutory obligation to employ such headlines | The absence of any other means of reporting the facts to the public | The payment of a supplementary charge by readers
exam: Whereas a short extract of a speech is circulated widely, select the course of verification most consistent with good practice. | Consultation of the full recording from an official source, including the passages adjacent | Repeated viewing of the same extract until it is memorised, together with its transcription by hand | Reliance upon the commentary appended beneath the extract | Enquiry of the account which circulated the extract
